1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,060
ADAM: OK Rosie, shall we try to get some ideas together for our presentation on diet and obesity?

2
00:00:08,060 --> 00:00:09,100
ROSIE: Sure.

3
00:00:09,100 --> 00:00:12,698
ADAM: I can talk about the experiment I did to see

4
00:00:12,779 --> 00:00:16,900
if people can tell the difference between real sugar and artificial sweeteners

5
00:00:16,900 --> 00:00:18,632
ROSIE: Where you gave people drinks

6
00:00:18,632 --> 00:00:21,020
with either sugar or artificial sweeteners

7
00:00:21,020 --> 00:00:23,250
and they had to say which they thought it was?

8
00:00:23,250 --> 00:00:27,562
ADAM: Yeah. It took me ages to decide exactly how I'd organise it,

9
00:00:27,562 --> 00:00:31,320
especially how I could make sure that people didn't know

10
00:00:31,320 --> 00:00:32,870
which drink I was giving them.

11
00:00:32,870 --> 00:00:38,265
It was hard to keep track of it all, especially as I had so many people doing it -

12
00:00:38,265 --> 00:00:43,060
I had to make sure I kept a proper record of what each person had had.

13
00:00:43,060 --> 00:00:45,900
ROSIE: So could most people tell the difference?

14
00:00:45,900 --> 00:00:50,590
ADAM: Yeah - I hadn't thought they would be able to, but most people could.

15
00:00:50,590 --> 00:00:55,780
ROSIE: Then there's that experiment I did measuring the fat content of nuts,

16
00:00:55,780 --> 00:01:00,570
to see if the nutritional information given on the packet was accurate.

17
00:01:00,570 --> 00:01:02,900
ADAM: The one where you ground up the nuts

18
00:01:02,900 --> 00:01:05,520
and mixed them with a chemical to absorb the fat?

19
00:01:05,520 --> 00:01:08,451
ROSIE: Yes. My results were a bit problematic -

20
00:01:08,451 --> 00:01:14,680
the fat content for that type of nut seemed much lower than it said on the package.

21
00:01:14,680 --> 00:01:17,500
But I reckon the package information was right.

22
00:01:17,500 --> 00:01:21,520
I think I should probably have ground up the nuts more than I did.

23
00:01:21,520 --> 00:01:26,230
It's possible that the scales for weighing the fat weren't accurate enough, too.

24
00:01:26,230 --> 00:01:32,560
I'd really like to try the experiment again some time.

25
00:01:32,560 --> 00:01:37,910
ADAM: So what can we say about helping people to lose weight?

26
00:01:37,910 --> 00:01:42,400
There's a lot we could say about what restaurants could do to reduce obesity.

27
00:01:42,400 --> 00:01:45,200
I read that the items at the start of a menu

28
00:01:45,200 --> 00:01:50,800
and the items at the end of a menu are much more likely to be chosen than the items in the middle.

29
00:01:50,800 --> 00:01:56,427
So, if you put the low-calorie items at the beginning and end of the menu,

30
00:01:56,427 --> 00:02:00,200
people will probably go for the food with fewer calories

31
00:02:00,200 --> 00:02:02,430
without even realising what they're doing.

32
00:02:02,430 --> 00:02:07,490
ROSIE: I think food manufacturers could do more to encourage healthy eating.

33
00:02:07,490 --> 00:02:08,090
ADAM: How?

34
00:02:08,090 --> 00:02:12,827
ROSIE: Well, when manufacturers put calorie counts of a food on the label,

35
00:02:12,827 --> 00:02:17,200
they're sometimes really confusing and I suspect they do it on purpose.

36
00:02:17,200 --> 00:02:23,180
Because food that's high in calories tastes better, and so they'll sell more.

37
00:02:23,180 --> 00:02:27,665
ADAM: Yeah, so if you look at the amount of calories in a pizza,

38
00:02:27,665 --> 00:02:30,800
they'll give you the calories per quarter pizza and you think

39
00:02:30,840 --> 00:02:32,790
oh that's not too bad.

40
00:02:32,790 --> 00:02:35,250
But who's going to eat a quarter pizza?

41
00:02:35,250 --> 00:02:36,280
ROSIE: Exactly.

42
00:02:36,280 --> 00:02:41,530
ADAM: I suppose another approach to this problem is to get people to exercise more.

43
00:02:41,530 --> 00:02:49,200
ROSIE: Right. In England, the current guidelines are for at least 30 minutes of brisk walking, five days a week.

44
00:02:49,200 --> 00:02:56,216
Now when you ask them, about 40% of men and 30% of women say they do this,

45
00:02:56,216 --> 00:03:01,994
but when you objectively measure the amount of walking they do with motion sensors,

46
00:03:01,994 --> 00:03:09,010
you find that only 6% of men and 4% of women do the recommended amount of exercise.

47
00:03:09,010 --> 00:03:12,260
ADAM: Mm, so you can see why obesity is growing.

48
00:03:12,260 --> 00:03:16,720
ROSIE: So how can people be encouraged to take more exercise?

49
00:03:16,720 --> 00:03:22,430
ADAM: Well, for example, think of the location of stairs in a train station.

50
00:03:22,430 --> 00:03:26,680
If people reach the stairs before they reach the escalator

51
00:03:26,680 --> 00:03:28,185
when they're leaving the station,

52
00:03:28,185 --> 00:03:30,870
they're more likely to take the stairs.

53
00:03:30,870 --> 00:03:34,060
And if you increase the width of the stairs

54
00:03:34,080 --> 00:03:37,030
you'll get more people using them at the same time.

55
00:03:37,030 --> 00:03:43,410
It's an unconscious process and influenced by minor modifications in their environment.

56
00:03:43,410 --> 00:03:47,100
ROSIE: Right. And it might not be a big change

57
00:03:47,100 --> 00:03:49,750
but if it happens every day, it all adds up.

58
00:03:49,750 --> 00:03:52,660
ADAM: Yes. But actually, I'm not sure

59
00:03:52,660 --> 00:03:55,460
if we should be talking about exercise in our presentation.

60
00:03:55,460 --> 00:03:58,470
ROSIE: Well, we've done quite a bit of reading about it.

61
00:03:58,470 --> 00:04:03,072
ADAM: I know, but it's going to mean we have a very wide focus,

62
00:04:03,072 --> 00:04:06,500
and our tutor did say that we need to focus on causes

63
00:04:06,500 --> 00:04:08,990
and solutions in terms of nutrition.

64
00:04:08,990 --> 00:04:13,800
ROSIE: I suppose so. And we've got plenty of information about that.

65
00:04:13,800 --> 00:04:17,100
OK, well that will be simpler.

66
00:04:17,100 --> 00:04:19,510
ADAM: So what shall we do now?

67
00:04:19,510 --> 00:04:22,850
We've still got half an hour before our next lecture.

68
00:04:22,850 --> 00:04:26,940
ROSIE: Let's think about what we're going to include and what will go where.

69
00:04:26,940 --> 00:04:29,620
Then we can decide what slides we need.

70
00:04:29,620 --> 00:04:32,000
ADAM: OK, fine.
